\chapter{Discussions in videos and documents}
\label{Discussions}

In FBF's video and document activities students discuss the material in
place: they mark a passage of the video or a region of the document and
comment on it, and others reply.
In videos the teacher can also pose questions at given times, which the
students answer.
None of this reaches Canvas, which gets a completion grade at most.
The [[discussions]] command lists these threads.

\section{How FBF stores a discussion}

We learned the structure by following the API's relationships from an
activity; \cref{discussion-model} shows what we found.
\begin{description}
\item[Activity and material.] A video or document activity has several
  tasks.
  One of them, a [[multimedia-tasks]] resource, has the [[material]]: a
  [[multimedia/material]], the same type for videos and documents.
\item[Threads.] The material has [[comments]], the opening posts of the
  students' threads, and [[questions]], the teacher's.
  Questions come in three types: [[open-questions]],
  [[multiple-choice-questions]] and [[scratch-off-questions]].
  A question has [[answers]], each with a [[score]].
  An open answer has a [[body]]; a choice answer has none, nor upvotes, but
  [[choices]], references to the question's choices
  ([[questions/choice]], each with a [[body]]).
  The choices come only as bare references; we fetch them in one request
  per material, as [[v1/questions/choices?filter[id]=...]].
\item[Replies.] Comments, questions and answers all have [[comments]] of
  their own, which are replies and may have replies in turn.
  The attribute [[comments-count]] tells whether there are any, which saves
  a request per post without replies.
\item[Position.] The material's [[annotations]] tie a thread to a place: an
  annotation's [[child]] is the comment or question, its [[parent]] a
  fragment.
  A [[video-fragments]] resource spans [[time-min]] to [[time-max]] seconds,
  a [[document-fragments]] resource a rectangle from [[x-min]], [[y-min]] to
  [[x-max]], [[y-max]].
\item[Authors.] Each post has a [[user]], whose [[name]] we get by asking
  for [[include=user]] with the posts.
\end{description}
One quirk shaped the code: FBF answers [[500]] when asked to include two
relationships at once ([[include=user,annotation]]), so we fetch the
annotations and fragments as lists of their own and join them ourselves.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\begin{verbatim}
activity --tasks--> multimedia-tasks --material--> multimedia/material
material --comments--> comments --comments--> replies --> ...
material --questions--> *-questions --answers--> *-answers
*-choice-questions/answers --choices--> questions/choice
material --annotations--> annotations: child = comment | question
                                       parent = video-/document-fragment
\end{verbatim}
\caption{The resources of a discussion and their relationships.}
\label{discussion-model}
\end{figure}

