\section{Fetching a discussion}

Fetching follows the model of \cref{discussion-model}.
Each list of posts comes with its authors ([[include=user]]), and a post's
replies are fetched only when its [[comments-count]] says there are any.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_posts(fbf, link, reply=False, choices=None):
    """Return the `Post`s at the relationship `link`, replies included."""
    document = fbf.get(link, include="user")
    users = users_of(fbf, document)
    return [make_post(resource, users, fetch_replies(fbf, resource),
                      reply=reply, choices=choices)
            for resource in document.resources]


def fetch_replies(fbf, resource):
    """Return the replies to a post `resource`, recursively."""
    if not attribute(resource, "comments-count"):
        return []
    return fetch_posts(fbf, related_link(resource, "comments"), reply=True)
@

A question's answers are posts below it, like replies, but the question's
replies proper (its [[comments]]) come too.
We put the answers first, since they are what the question asked for.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_question(fbf, resource, users, choices=None):
    """Return the `Post` of a question with its answers and replies."""
    answers = fetch_posts(fbf, related_link(resource, "answers"),
                          choices=choices)
    return make_post(resource, users,
                     answers + fetch_replies(fbf, resource))


def related_link(resource, name):
    """Return the `related` link of the relationship `name`."""
    return (relationship(resource, name).get("links") or {})["related"]
<<imports>>=
from fbf.schema import relationship
@

A material's threads are its comments and its questions, each placed by the
annotations.
Opening comments become posts with their replies; questions go through
[[fetch_question]], which adds the answers.

<<functions>>=
def material_threads(fbf, material, activity):
    """Return the `Thread`s on a `material`, sorted by position."""
    lists = {name: fbf.get(related_link(material, name)).resources
             for name in ("annotations", "fragments")}
    places = anchors(lists["annotations"], lists["fragments"])
    comments = fbf.get(related_link(material, "comments"), include="user")
    questions = fbf.get(related_link(material, "questions"), include="user")
    choices = fetch_choices(fbf, questions.resources)
    threads = [Thread(activity, position(places.get(resource["id"])),
                      make_post(resource, users_of(fbf, comments),
                                fetch_replies(fbf, resource)))
               for resource in comments.resources]
    threads += [Thread(activity, position(places.get(resource["id"])),
                       fetch_question(fbf, resource,
                                      users_of(fbf, questions),
                                      choices))
                for resource in questions.resources]
    return sorted(threads, key=thread_order)


@

The authors should come with the posts, through [[include=user]].
They do for comments, but the question and answer endpoints ignore the
parameter and include nobody, so every question and answer would be
anonymous.
We therefore take the included users and fetch the missing ones by id
([[get_by_ids]] of \cref{API}).

<<functions>>=
def users_of(fbf, document):
    """Map the ids of the authors in `document` to user resources."""
    users = {id: user for (kind, id), user in document.included.items()
             if kind == "users"}
    missing = {related_id(resource, "user")
               for resource in document.resources} - set(users)
    users.update(fbf.get_by_ids("v1/users", missing))
    return users
@

The choices of all choice questions on a material are fetched together, by
their ids.

<<functions>>=
def fetch_choices(fbf, questions):
    """Map choice ids to the choices of the choice `questions`."""
    return fbf.get_by_ids(
        "v1/questions/choices",
        [reference["id"] for question in questions
         if question["type"] in CHOICE_QUESTIONS
         for reference in relationship(question, "choices").get("data")
                          or []])
@

Sorting by position compares strings, which would put [[10:00]] before
[[9:00]].
We sort by the numbers in the position instead, then by time of posting.

<<imports>>=
import re
<<functions>>=
def thread_order(thread):
    """Sort key for threads: numeric position, then creation time."""
    numbers = [float(n) for n in re.findall(r"[0-9.]+", thread.position)
               if n != "."]
    return (numbers, thread.post.created)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_thread_order_is_numeric():
  late = Thread("a", "10:00--10:05", Post("comment", "", "1", ""))
  early = Thread("a", "9:00--9:05", Post("comment", "", "2", ""))
  assert sorted([late, early], key=thread_order) == [early, late]
@

Finally, an activity's materials hang off its tasks, which come as bare
references (see [[related]] in \cref{API}), so we ask for them with
[[include=tasks.material]].

<<functions>>=
def activity_threads(fbf, activity_id, name=None):
    """Return the `Thread`s of all materials of an activity.

    `name` labels the threads; it defaults to the activity's title.
    """
    document = fbf.get(f"v1/activities/{activity_id}",
                       include="tasks.material")
    name = name or attribute(document.resource, "title")
    return [thread
            for (kind, _), material in document.included.items()
            if kind == "multimedia/material"
            for thread in material_threads(fbf, material, name)]
@


\section{Output}

The output follows [[canvaslms]]: one row per item, fields separated by the
delimiter, tab by default, so that it pipes into [[cut]], [[sort]] or a
spreadsheet.
By default a row is a thread: the activity, the position, the kind, the
author, when, how many posts below it, the upvotes, and the text.
With [[--long]] every post gets a row, with its depth in the thread, which
is what reading a discussion needs.
Line breaks in a body would break the rows, so we fold them into spaces.

<<functions>>=
def thread_rows(threads, long=False):
    """Yield output rows (lists of strings) for `threads`."""
    for thread in threads:
        posts = walk(thread.post) if long else [(0, thread.post)]
        for depth, post in posts:
            yield [thread.activity, thread.position,
                   post.kind,
                   post.author, post.created,
                   str(depth) if long else str(post.count()),
                   str(post.upvotes),
                   "" if post.score is None else str(post.score),
                   " ".join(post.body.split())]


def walk(post, depth=0):
    """Yield `(depth, post)` for `post` and all posts below it."""
    yield depth, post
    for reply in post.replies:
        yield from walk(reply, depth + 1)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_thread_rows_short_and_long():
  reply = Post("reply", "Bo", "t2", "Me\ntoo")
  thread = Thread("Week 1", "0:10--0:20",
                  Post("comment", "Ada", "t1", "Why?", 3, replies=[reply]))
  assert list(thread_rows([thread])) == [
    ["Week 1", "0:10--0:20", "comment", "Ada", "t1", "1", "3", "", "Why?"]]
  assert [row[2:6] + row[8:] for row in thread_rows([thread], long=True)] \
    == [["comment", "Ada", "t1", "0", "Why?"],
        ["reply", "Bo", "t2", "1", "Me too"]]
@


\section{The command}

The command takes the [[canvaslms]] assignment options, finds the FBF
activities of the selected assignments in their course round
(\cref{Canvas}), and lists the threads of each.
Assignments that are not FBF's are skipped, so [[-c]] alone lists every
discussion in a course; so are those not yet opened in FBF this round, with
a note on standard error.
The threads are labelled with the Canvas assignment name, which is the name
the user selected them by.

<<imports>>=
import json
import sys
import canvaslms.cli
import canvaslms.cli.assignments
import canvaslms.cli.cache
import canvaslms.cli.login
from fbf import canvas as fbfcanvas
<<functions>>=
def discussions_command(config, canvas, args):
    """List the discussion threads of the selected FBF assignments."""
    assignments = [a for a in
                   canvaslms.cli.assignments.process_assignment_option(
                       canvas, args)
                   if fbfcanvas.placement_code(a)]
    if not assignments:
        raise canvaslms.cli.EmptyListError("No FeedbackFruits assignments")
    hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)
    threads = []
    for assignment, fbf, activity in fbfcanvas.fbf_activities(
            canvaslms.cli.cache.normalize_hostname(hostname), token,
            assignments):
        if activity:
            threads += activity_threads(fbf, activity, assignment.name)
        else:
            print(f"{assignment.name}: not opened in FeedbackFruits "
                  "this course round, skipped", file=sys.stderr)
    <<output [[threads]] as [[args]] say>>
@

JSON output keeps the reply tree, which rows flatten.

<<output [[threads]] as [[args]] say>>=
if args.json:
    json.dump([dataclasses.asdict(thread) for thread in threads],
              sys.stdout, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
    print()
else:
    delimiter = getattr(args, "delimiter", "\t")
    for row in thread_rows(threads, long=args.long):
        print(delimiter.join(row))
@

<<functions>>=
def add_command(subp):
    """Add the `discussions` command to `subp`."""
    parser = subp.add_parser(
        "discussions",
        help="List discussion threads in FBF video and document activities",
        description="List the threads of FeedbackFruits video and document "
                    "activities: activity, position, kind, author, time, "
                    "posts below (or depth with -l), upvotes, score, text.")
    canvaslms.cli.assignments.add_assignment_option(parser, ungraded=False)
    parser.add_argument("-l", "--long", action="store_true",
                        help="one row per post instead of per thread")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true",
                        help="print threads with their replies as JSON")
    parser.set_defaults(func=discussions_command)
@
